cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  aligncal: relative sensor alignment from flight time series, with the
  observability of each alignment degree of freedom reported before the estimate
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-29
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Takes time-series angular rates from two or more attitude-relevant sensors
  with a declared mounting geometry and estimates the relative misalignment
  rotations with confidence intervals. The first output of every call is an
  observability verdict: which alignment degrees of freedom the observed motion
  actually excites, computed before the estimate it gates. The structural
  result the package reports is that the observability Gramian of a rate-pair
  alignment problem and the normalised rate second moment share eigenvectors
  exactly, so the least observable alignment direction is the most excited
  rotation axis and a vehicle that rotated about one fixed axis cannot have its
  alignment about that axis estimated at any data volume. A bare numerical rank
  test on the measured Gramian does not see this, because the reference
  sensor's own noise fills the null space; numpy.linalg.matrix_rank reported
  rank 3 on 5 of 5 records that support 2, and a declared noise floor is
  therefore part of the verdict. The analytic baselines are a batch
  least-squares Wahba-type solution restricted to the floor-clearing subspace,
  cross-checked against Markley's SVD solution, and a multiplicative extended
  Kalman filter scored against it; they agree to 0.052809 of the Cramer-Rao one
  sigma on well-conditioned records. Validation is level 2 over 864 records:
  the standardised recovery error against the Cramer-Rao bound has mean
  -0.073846 and standard deviation 0.959812 against a standard normal, with no
  score above 2.7673 in 2290, and the Gauss-Newton iteration converged on 864
  of 864. Four results are unfavourable and are published as measured. The
  regularised analytic baseline beats every learned configuration on the
  held-out short-record population, 0.697326 against 0.760152 milliradians, on
  all four excitation profiles. With a misalignment of 2 milliradians injected
  entirely along a single-axis record's own rotation axis, the sharpest learned
  configuration claims that component with a reported one sigma of 14.3540
  arcseconds against a truth of 412.5296 arcseconds, 29.0693 of its own
  standard deviations, with two-sigma coverage of 0.0000 over 120 records,
  while the batch solution withholds the direction and reports an infinite
  bound on all 120; along the best-observed direction of the same records the
  same models cover at or above the nominal 0.954500. The recursive filter is
  overconfident in the unsupported direction too, covering on 0.591837 of
  records. An unconstrained SVD solution picks up a gauge rotation with a
  median of 1.5355 radians in the null direction and that rotation contaminates
  the directions the record does support. Sixteen controls were run, ten of
  which must fire and ten of which did, six of which must not and none of which
  did. All numbers are measured against a seeded synthetic generator whose
  equations are printed in its module docstring; no flight data is used
  anywhere, and the only sensor error modelled is white noise. Research grade.
  Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace
  use. An observable alignment is not a calibrated one.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - alignment
  - observability
  - attitude
  - gyroscope
  - calibration
  - kalman-filter
  - wahba
  - aerospace
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wahba
        given-names: "G."
    title: "A least squares estimate of satellite attitude"
    journal: "SIAM Review"
    volume: 7
    issue: 3
    year: 1965
    notes: >-
      The minimisation of sum w_i |b_i - R r_i|^2 over rotations. Linearised
      about the identity it becomes equation (14) of aligncal.batch, and the
      matrix of the resulting normal equations is the Gramian of equation (8).
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Markley
        given-names: "F. L."
    title: >-
      Attitude determination using vector observations and the singular value
      decomposition
    journal: "Journal of the Astronautical Sciences"
    volume: 36
    issue: 3
    year: 1988
    notes: >-
      The SVD solution of equation (17), used here as an independent
      cross-check on the 3x3 normal equations. It has no mechanism to withhold
      an unobservable degree of freedom, and the gauge rotation it returns
      there is measured in control C13.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Lefferts
        given-names: "E. J."
      - family-names: Markley
        given-names: "F. L."
      - family-names: Shuster
        given-names: "M. D."
    title: "Kalman filtering for spacecraft attitude estimation"
    journal: "Journal of Guidance, Control, and Dynamics"
    volume: 5
    issue: 5
    year: 1982
    notes: >-
      The multiplicative error-state formulation and the reset of equation
      (22), which is what makes aligncal.recursive an MEKF rather than an
      additive filter.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Markley
        given-names: "F. L."
      - family-names: Crassidis
        given-names: "J. L."
    title: >-
      Fundamentals of Spacecraft Attitude Determination and Control
    publisher:
      name: "Springer"
    year: 2014
    notes: >-
      Section 2.9 for the small-rotation parameterisation and equation (2.113)
      for Rodrigues' formula; section 6.2 for the sensor-alignment state and
      the convention of a misalignment applied multiplicatively on a declared
      mounting.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Kay
        given-names: "S. M."
    title: >-
      Fundamentals of Statistical Signal Processing: Estimation Theory
    publisher:
      name: "Prentice Hall"
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      Section 3.9, the Cramer-Rao bound for the general linear Gaussian model,
      which is equation (12). The bound constrains unbiased estimators only,
      which is why the regularised variant of equation (18) is allowed below
      it and why its bias is reported beside it.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Horn
        given-names: "R. A."
      - family-names: Johnson
        given-names: "C. R."
    title: "Matrix Analysis"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Cambridge University Press"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Theorem 6.1.1, Gershgorin's circle theorem, and Theorem 4.2.2, the
      Rayleigh quotient. Together they are the independent bracket of equation
      (13) on the smallest Gramian eigenvalue, derived from mathematics the
      eigensolver does not use.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Gershgorin
        given-names: "S. A."
    title: "Uber die Abgrenzung der Eigenwerte einer Matrix"
    journal: "Izvestiya Akademii Nauk SSSR, Seriya Matematicheskaya"
    volume: 6
    year: 1931
    notes: >-
      The original statement of the circle theorem used in equation (13).
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Bar-Shalom
        given-names: "Y."
      - family-names: Li
        given-names: "X.-R."
      - family-names: Kirubarajan
        given-names: "T."
    title: "Estimation with Applications to Tracking and Navigation"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      Section 5.2, the Joseph-form covariance update of equation (21), used
      because the short form loses symmetry and positive definiteness over the
      thousands of updates a rate record contains.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: James
        given-names: "W."
      - family-names: Stein
        given-names: "C."
    title: "Estimation with quadratic loss"
    journal: >-
      Proceedings of the Fourth Berkeley Symposium on Mathematical Statistics
      and Probability
    volume: 1
    year: 1961
    notes: >-
      The bias-variance trade behind the regularised estimator of equation
      (18) and behind the only mechanism by which a learned estimator can beat
      an unbiased solution on a short record.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Lehmann
        given-names: "E. L."
      - family-names: Casella
        given-names: "G."
    title: "Theory of Point Estimation"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Springer"
    year: 1998
    notes: >-
      Section 4.1, Bayes risk. Cited for what is NOT claimed: equation (18) is
      the posterior mean under a Gaussian prior matched in second moment, the
      generated population's prior is not Gaussian, so no domination theorem
      applies and the measured behaviour is a measurement rather than a
      consequence.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Pedregosa
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Varoquaux
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Gramfort
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python"
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 12
    year: 2011
    notes: >-
      RandomForestRegressor and Ridge. aligncal.learned reads the individual
      ensemble members to form the spread it reports as its uncertainty
      output, which is why scikit-learn is pinned below 1.10.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Virtanen
        given-names: "P."
      - family-names: Gommers
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: Oliphant
        given-names: "T. E."
    title: "SciPy 1.0: fundamental algorithms for scientific computing in Python"
    journal: "Nature Methods"
    volume: 17
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      scipy.special.erf, used so that every nominal coverage figure reported
      here is computed rather than written by hand, and
      scipy.spatial.transform.Rotation, used in the test suite as an
      independent cross-check on aligncal.geometry.
